As I've been working on this I found out that it pretty much affects anything that is not application/javascript for script tag, the reason behind appears to be in the way that HTMLAsset sets the script tag. 

Now by default if `script type="application/javascript" || script type="text/javascript"` on HTMLAsset postProcess what would happen by default is the removal of the type property of the script tag
```
// Delete "type" attribute, since CSS and JS are the defaults.
// Unless it's application/ld+json
```
however for types "application/js" or "application/ts", or "application/coffee" or for values not set in the SCRIPT_TYPES array it will reuse the generated.meta.node.attrs.type value which for all TypeScript and coffeeScript assets are either 

```
const SCRIPT_TYPES = {
  'application/javascript': 'js',
  'text/javascript': 'js',
  'application/json': false,
  'application/ld+json': 'jsonld',
  'text/html': false
};
```

One way to fix this would be to do the following:

```
if (rendition.isMain) {
  node.content = rendition.value;
  // Code modification here in postProcess HTMLAsset
  // lines 268:273
  if (node.attrs && rendition.type === 'js') {
    node.attrs.type = "application/javascript"; 
  }
}
```

With this I managed to run through the default behaviour of removing the property type of the script tag, and so the html file would end up looking like 

```
<!doctype html>
<html>
    <body>
        <script>
          function log(t) {
            console.log(t + "hello");
          }
          log("bouh");
        </script>
    </body>
</html>
```

That is one possible solution.

Any thoughts?
